            "text": "{\n  \"primary_meaning\": \"Conformity to God's character, standard, and will, encompassing both a right relational and legal standing before God and moral uprightness in conduct.\",\n  \"primary_basis\": [\n    \"Genesis 15:6\",\n    \"Romans 1:17\",\n    \"Romans 3:21-26\",\n    \"Romans 4:3\",\n    \"2 Corinthians 5:21\"\n  ],\n  \"alternatives\": [\n    {\n      \"meaning\": \"Imputed righteousness: a legal declaration where Christ's alien righteousness is credited to the believer by faith, apart from works (historic Protestant emphasis).\",\n      \"basis\": [\n        \"Philippians 3:9\",\n        \"Romans 4:4-5\"\n      ],\n      \"prominence\": \"dominant\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"meaning\": \"Infused righteousness: intrinsic moral transformation and holiness imparted to the believer through grace and the sacraments, making them inherently just (historic Roman Catholic emphasis).\",\n      \"basis\": [\n        \"James 2:24\",\n        \"Romans 5:19\",\n        \"Matthew 5:20\"\n      ],\n      \"prominence\": \"dominant\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"meaning\": \"Covenant faithfulness: God's loyal adherence to His promises to Israel and the world, and correspondingly, a person's status as a faithful member of God's covenant family (emphasized in the New Perspective on Paul).\",\n      \"basis\": [\n        \"Romans 3:3-5\",\n        \"Psalm 143:1\",\n        \"Isaiah 51:5-6\"\n      ],\n      \"prominence\": \"common\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"meaning\": \"Social justice and equity: right relationships expressed through ethical treatment of others, fair societal systems, and advocacy for the poor and marginalized (Old Testament Prophetic tradition).\",\n      \"basis\": [\n        \"Amos 5:24\",\n        \"Isaiah 1:17\",\n        \"Micah 6:8\"\n      ],\n      \"prominence\": \"common\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"has_settled_consensus\": false,\n  \"confidence\": \"high\",\n  \"notes\": \"While there is general agreement that righteousness denotes alignment with God, the term lacks a singular settled theological definition across Christianity. This is due to profound historical schisms (e.g., the Reformation debate between imputed and infused righteousness) and modern biblical scholarship (e.g., the New Perspective on Paul, which views righteousness primarily through the lens of covenant inclusion rather than individualized legal or moral categories).\"\n}",
